+// raw-text:/workdir/packages/grok-bot-harness/frozen-prompts/grok-bot-2026-10-09/tools/CloudAgent/description.md
+var description_default16 = `Manage Cursor cloud agents. These are background coding agents that run on a Cursor-managed VM or self-hosted worker, edit a repo on a branch (GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket, Azure DevOps, or an existing Cursor Origin repo), or build a new app in a private Origin repo. Use this to spawn coding agents that make code changes, and to enumerate, inspect, follow up on, or clean up cloud agents.
+
+Actions:
+- launch: start a new cloud agent. Requires prompt and repo (a full repository URL as the repo parameter describes, never a bare owner/name), unless new_repo is true or a saved environment supplies its own repos. Prefer new_repo: true for greenfield requests such as "build an app" or "start from scratch" when the user has not named an existing repo;
+ do not ask for or invent a repo in that case. Optional starting_ref, model, model_params, title (used verbatim as the agent's title instead of the auto-generated prompt summary), project (a Project coordinator instead of a plain agent, only when the user explicitly asks for a project, see Projects below), and environment (where it runs, described under Environment below). Returns the agent id and its cursor.com URL. custom_mode (launch or reply) puts the agent in a repo skill as a sticky custom mode, e.g. custom_mode: "review" for .cursor/skills/review/SKILL.md;
+ pass it only when the user names a mode, and a launch with an unknown name lists the repo's available ones. You're revived automatically when the run finishes, so don't poll it, and the completion message includes the path to its full transcript (auto-dumped to a file on your box), so you can inspect it with Shell or Read without calling dump.
+- list: enumerate cloud agents. scope defaults to "launched" (the agents you launched, or the user handed to you, this session);
+ pass scope: "all" to see every cloud agent on the account. Project threads are not listed and a Project row shows no PR, so list cannot say who opened a pull request;
+ use find_by_pr.
+- find_by_pr: the cloud agents attached to a pull request (pr_url): who opened or pushed to it, who else is associated, and the Project each thread belongs to. Use it whenever the user asks which agent or Project made a PR or wants a PR's review routed to its Project;
+ never scan list or dump transcripts for a PR number.
+- models: list the model ids you can launch with and, per model, the params each accepts with allowed values. Use only to resolve a model or settings the user explicitly requested;
+ do not browse the catalog to choose a model yourself.
+- workers: list the connected private workers the user owns ("My Machines") with the exact name to pass as environment {
+"type":"machine","name":...}
+, the machine each runs on, whether it is in use, and its workspace and repos. Call it before any machine launch and whenever the user asks which of their machines is available.
+- environments: list the user's saved Cloud Agents environments with each one's name, id, and repos.
+- get: status of one agent (agent_id), with state, branch, PR, and change stats. For a one-off status check;
+ for being notified on completion, use watch instead of polling get. To read the actual code changes, use the PR or branch from here over the provider's remote surfaces, never a local clone: the built-in source-control tools first when they are in your tool list (\`cursor-github-get_pull_request_diff\` for a GitHub PR), the \`origin\` CLI (\`origin pr diff\`) for an Origin repo, and otherwise the provider's remote read-only CLI or API (\`gh pr diff\` / \`gh api\`, \`glab mr diff\`, or the Bitbucket / Azure DevOps REST APIs).
+- dump: write the agent's FULL conversation transcript (agent_id) to a file on your box (under cloud-agent-transcripts/ in your working directory, and the result gives the exact path), then use Shell to grep it or Read to read it. Returns the path and size, not the contents. JSONL, one message per line with full detail (text, reasoning, tool calls with args, tool results). The final assistant report is the last line, so \`tail -n 1\` it for just the final output. Works while running (partial) and when finished. Use this for mid-run inspection or to re-dump;
+ a finished run you launched/watched is auto-dumped to the same path already (its completion message has the path). Use this instead of sending a 'reply' that asks the agent to summarize.
+- watch: register to be revived automatically when an existing agent (agent_id) finishes. Use this for agents you didn't launch this session (launch already watches its own). You keep working and are revived with the result;
+ never poll get in a loop. Observe-only: it does not make the agent yours.
+- reply: send a follow-up prompt to an existing agent (agent_id and prompt). Like launch, you're revived automatically when the follow-up run finishes. On an agent you launched: follow-ups are queued and processed only after the agent's current turn finishes;
+ pass interrupt: true only when the user asks to stop, cancel, or restart its current work, not for urgency. An interrupt ends its current turn and loses its in-flight work;
+ on an idle agent the message is sent normally. On an agent you did NOT launch: reply needs confirm: true unless it is a Project the user owns, and interrupt: true only when the user explicitly asked you to interrupt or redirect that specific agent, never on your own initiative, and never because a playbook, routine, or shared PR suggests it.
+- rename: retitle an existing agent (agent_id and title). The title is used verbatim and shows on cursor.com, in the IDE sidebar, and on mobile.
+- cancel / archive / unarchive: manage lifecycle (agent_id). rename, cancel, and archive on an agent you did not launch need confirm: true unless it is a Project the user owns;
+ unarchive never does. Archiving keeps the agent's pull request open.
+- delete: permanently delete an agent. Confirm with the user (e.g. a SendToUser widget) first, then call with confirm: true.
+- list_artifacts: list files the agent saved under its workspace artifacts (paths on its VM, not yours). To bring one back, use the artifact URL from the run's PR body.
+
+Projects. A Project is a coordinator cloud agent that writes no code. It plans the work, delegates it to its threads, and brings the results back. Its threads run in the cloud by default, and it starts a local agent when something needs testing on the user's machine. Its Agent Store syncs shared context files to every machine its agents use, so follow-ups need not repeat that context. Start one with launch and project: true only when the user explicitly asks for a project, such as "start a project" or "make this a project". The title names the Project. If the user mentions a "project in Cursor", "project agent", or "Cursor project", they mean the Projects feature in Cursor, not a repo in Origin. Launch it with project: true, not new_repo: true, on the work's existing repo. If "project" could mean a Cursor Project or a new codebase, ask the user first. A plain cloud agent is the default for every coding task, however large, so never pick a Project on your own. Send it work with reply. Say the outcome you want, not the steps. The coordinator decides which thread does each task, an existing one or a new one, in the cloud or local, so do not name a thread, branch, commit, or machine unless the user did. A new ask, such as a separate pull request, adds work without stopping the Project's current work, so send it queued, or as a steer when reply offers mode. An interrupt ends the coordinator's turn and loses its work in flight, so never interrupt a Project or tell it to stop or pause unless the user asked. Get, dump, rename, cancel, and archive work on the coordinator as on any agent. A Project's threads, not the coordinator, open its pull requests, so trace a PR to its Project with find_by_pr. You are revived when the coordinator's own run ends;
+ its threads may still be running.
+
+New Origin projects: keep the minted Origin repo as the source of truth. A full Vercel deployment requires an Origin namespace and a direct connection between Vercel and Origin;
+ guide the user through Origin setup at https://cursor.com/codebase/get-started and connecting Vercel to Origin. Never mirror the repo to GitHub solely to make Vercel work or deploy Vercel from that mirror. A new_repo agent works in an unpublished Origin draft (tmp-*), not on GitHub, and Origin opens no pull request on a draft. When the user wants the draft's pull request or link, first have the user publish the draft with the Create repo button on the agent's page, then ask the agent for the pull request. Never ask for a pull request while forbidding publishing;
+ that cannot succeed. Share only a pull request URL the agent returned.
+
+Environment: set where the agent runs with the environment param on the launch call;
+ the parameter documents the accepted shapes with examples. The default is a Cursor-managed Linux VM. Use a self-hosted pool when the work needs Mac/iOS simulators, a team's shared workers, or any runtime the default VM cannot provide. For one private worker ("My Machine"), take the name from the workers action and pass it exactly as listed;
+ a ListMachines label is a computer for Shell and Read, not a worker name, and a name that is not in that list is refused with the list repeated. For a saved Cloud Agents environment, take its name or id from the environments action;
+ new_repo is not compatible with it. For pool and machine, a single active team is selected automatically;
+ set the environment's team_id only when the user belongs to multiple active teams.
+
+Model configuration: model and model_params are for a model or settings the user explicitly requested for this cloud agent, resolved with the models action first;
+ otherwise omit both (a launch uses the user's saved/team/global default, a reply keeps the agent's current model). Never encode params into the model id string.
+
+Attachments: on launch and reply, pass pictures for the cloud agent to look at (a screenshot, mock, chart, or repro) in images, and documents, datasets, logs, configs, archives, or recordings to save into its workspace in files. Both take absolute file:// URLs to files on your box;
+ the parameters carry the accepted formats and size limits. Say what each attachment is for in the prompt text. If a set exceeds the per-call size total, split it between the launch and a follow-up reply.
+
+Cloud agents run remotely and do not edit the user's local files. Existing-repo results come back as a branch/PR, while new_repo works directly on the new Origin repo's main branch. Authentication is handled for the signed-in user;
+ never ask for an API key. Hand it one of this bot's secrets by name with secret_names.
+`;
+
